Below is a **complete, self‑contained example** that shows how to add an `AudioCapture` (the *non‑Component* class) into your **GameMode** in Unreal Engine 5 using C++.

---

## 1. What you need first

| Item | Why it’s needed |
|------|-----------------|
| **Header files** – `AudioCapture.h`, `AudioCapture.cpp` and the engine’s core headers (`Engine/Source/MetaClass/...`) are already available when you compile for UE5. |
| **A GameMode** subclass (or extend an existing one). |
| **An AudioCapture instance** created from the global `AAudioCapture` object (or via `CreateAudioCapture`). |
| **A way to expose the capture** to other modules (e.g., play sounds, stop recording, etc.). |

> **Note:** In UE5 the “AudioCapture” component lives under `UObject`. The non‑component version is just a plain `UAudioCapture*` that you can obtain from the global `AAudioCapture` singleton or by calling `CreateAudioCapture()`. It works exactly like the component but without the extra UI / event system.

---

## 2. Minimal GameMode implementation

```cpp
// AudioCaptureGameMode.cpp
#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "GameMode/GameMode.cs.h"   // <-- only if you have a .cs file; otherwise use pure C++
#include "MyGameMode.generated.h"

using namespace U;

/**
 * MyGameMode – Example GameMode that captures audio.
 */
UCLASS()
class MYPROJECT_API AMyGameMode : public AGameModeBase
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    /** Called at start of each frame – we do nothing special here,
     *         but this is where you could register listeners, etc. */
    virtual void BeginPlay() override;

    /** Called every frame – useful for processing live input. */
    virtual void Update(float DeltaTime) override;

private:
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣  Global reference to the audio capture device.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    AAudioCapture* T_CaptureInstance = nullptr;

    // Optional: a simple timer to stop recording after N seconds.
    USTRING& T_StopRecordingTimer = Timer(0);

public:
    /** Create the AudioCapture instance once during level load. */
    void InitializeAudioCapture();

    /** Call from PlayMode's BeginPlay to ensure everything is ready. */
    void InitializeAudioCapture();
};

```

### 2.1 Initializing the capture

Add the following methods to `MyGameMode`:

```cpp
void AMyGameMode::InitializeAudioCapture()
{
    // Option A – let the engine create it automatically:
    // auto* capture = Cast<AAudioCapture>(GetWorld()->GetGlobalPlayerController()->GetAudioCapture());
    // But the simplest is to ask the global singleton:

    T_CaptureInstance = AAudioCapture::CreateAudioCapture(); // returns pointer to new UAudioCapture*

    if (!T_CaptureInstance)
    {
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Error, TEXT("Failed to create AudioCapture"));
        return;
    }

    // OPTIONAL: set up basic properties (sample rate, format, etc.)
    T_CaptureInstance->SetSampleRate(48000);
    T_CaptureInstance->SetFormat(AudioFormatDefault); // or whatever you need

    // If you want to stop recording after X seconds, start a timer:
    SetTimer(T_StopRecordingTimer, &AMyGameMode::OnStopRecordingTimer, this, EQTIMER_NORMAL);
}

// Timer callback – called roughly every frame while recording.
void AMyGameMode::OnStopRecordingTimer()
{
    if (T_CaptureInstance && T_CaptureInstance->IsRecording())
    {
        T_CaptureInstance->StopRecording();
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Warning, TEXT("AudioCapture stopped"));
    }
}
```

> **Explanation**
> - `AAudioCapture::CreateAudioCapture()` creates a fresh instance attached to the default audio device.  
> - You can also retrieve an existing one with `GetGlobalPlayerController()->GetAudioCapture()` if you prefer per‑player capture.  
> - `IsRecording()` tells us whether the device is currently streaming data.  

---

## 3. Hooking the capture into gameplay

You may want to expose a function that plays a sound through the captured stream, e.g.:

```cpp
// Inside AMyGameMode (optional)

void AMyGameMode::PlayCapturedSound(const FString& SoundPath)
{
    if (!T_CaptureInstance || !T_CaptureInstance->IsRecording())
    {
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Warning, TEXT("No active AudioCapture or not recording"));
        return;
    }

    // Build a temporary buffer containing the path (UE5 uses FCodecBuffer)
    FCodecBuffer* Buffer = FCodecBuffer::CreateFromPath(SoundPath, false, true, false);
    if (!Buffer)
    {
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Error, TEXT("Failed to create codec buffer for %s"), SoundPath.c_str());
        return;
    }

    // Use the capture's play method:
    T_CaptureInstance->PlayBuffer(Buffer);
}
```

Call `PlayCapturedSound("MySound.mp3")` wherever you need to replay something recorded earlier.

---

## 4. Full source files (for copy‑paste)

### `MyGameMode.generated.h`

```cpp
#pragma once

#include "GameMode/GameMode.h"

UCLASS()
class MYPROJECT_API AMyGameMode : public AGameModeBase
{
    GENERATED_BODY()
};
```

### `AudioCaptureGameMode.cpp`

```cpp
#include "AudioCaptureGameMode.cpp"
#include "MyGameMode.generated.h"

AMyGameMode::AMyGameMode()
{
    // No special constructor work needed.
}

void AMyGameMode::InitializeAudioCapture()
{
    // Create the capture instance.
    T_CaptureInstance = AAudioCapture::CreateAudioCapture();

    if (!T_CaptureInstance)
    {
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Error, TEXT("Failed to create AudioCapture"));
        return;
    }

    // Basic configuration (adjust as required).
    T_CaptureInstance->SetSampleRate(48000);
    T_CaptureInstance->SetFormat(AudioFormatDefault); // default is usually fine

    // Start a timer to stop recording after 10 seconds (example).
    SetTimer(T_StopRecordingTimer, &AMyGameMode::OnStopRecordingTimer, this, EQTIMER_NORMAL);
}

void AMyGameMode::BeginPlay()
{
    Super::BeginPlay();
    // Ensure capture is ready before first frame runs.
    InitializeAudioCapture();
}

void AMyGameMode::Update(float DeltaTime)
{
    Super::Update(DeltaTime);
    // You could poll IsRecording() here if you need per‑frame logic.
}

// Timer callback
void AMyGameMode::OnStopRecordingTimer()
{
    if (T_CaptureInstance && T_CaptureInstance->IsRecording())
    {
        T_CaptureInstance->StopRecording();
        UE_LOG(LogTemp, Warning, TEXT("AudioCapture stopped"));
    }
}
```

---

## 5. How to build & run

1. **Create a new C++ module** (or add to an existing one) named `AudioCaptureGameMode`.
2. Paste the two `.cpp/.h` files above into its folder.
3. Add `AudioCaptureGameMode` to your project’s **GameMode** list (if you haven’t already):
   ```bash
   Edit → Project Settings → Module → AudioCaptureGameMode → Checked
   ```
4. Compile with the usual UE5 workflow (`Ctrl+Shift+B`).
5. In your **Level**’s `GameMode` (or the main `GameMode`), make sure `BeginPlay` calls `InitializeAudioCapture()` (as shown).

Now every time the game starts, a fresh `UAudioCapture*` will be allocated on the default audio device and ready to record. When you call `PlayCapturedSound(...)` you’ll get the raw PCM data back, which you can feed into any audio player or save to disk.

---

## 6. Quick FAQ

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| **Do I need to attach the capture to a specific device?** | By default `CreateAudioCapture()` picks the first available hardware device. You can later change it via `SetDeviceID()` if you know the device index. |
| **Can I reuse the same instance across many GameModes?** | Yes – `AAudioCapture` is a global singleton, so creating it once (outside of GameMode) is enough. Just keep the pointer alive until you stop recording. |
| **How do I stop recording globally?** | Call `T_CaptureInstance->StopRecording()` manually or rely on the timer. |
| **What about thread safety?** | `UAudioCapture` is not thread‑safe. All operations should happen on the main thread (which is what `Update` does). |
| **I heard there’s a newer API called `AudioCaptureComponent`.** | That component wraps the underlying `UAudioCapture`. Using the non‑component directly gives you more low‑level control and avoids unnecessary overhead. |

---

### TL;DR Code snippet (everything you need)

```cpp
// In your GameMode header (only the relevant part)
UCLASS()
class MYPROJECT_API AMyGameMode : public AGameModeBase
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    AAudioCapture* T_CaptureInstance = nullptr;
    USTRING& T_StopRecordingTimer = Timer(0);

    void InitializeAudioCapture();
    void BeginPlay();
    void Update(float DeltaTime);
};

// Implementation (AudioCaptureGameMode.cpp)
#include "MyGameMode.generated.h"
#include "AudioCapture.h"   // <-- bring in the non‑component class

void AMyGameMode::InitializeAudioCapture()
{
    T_CaptureInstance = AAudioCapture::CreateAudioCapture();
    if (!T_CaptureInstance) { UE_LOG(LogTemp, Error, TEXT("CreateAudioCapture failed")); return; }
    T_CaptureInstance->SetSampleRate(48000);
    SetTimer(T_StopRecordingTimer, &AMyGameMode::OnStopRecordingTimer, this, EQTIMER_NORMAL);
}

void AMyGameMode::OnStopRecordingTimer()
{
    if (T_CaptureInstance && T_CaptureInstance->IsRecording())
    {
        T_CaptureInstance->StopRecording();
    }
}

void AMyGameMode::BeginPlay()
{
    Super::BeginPlay();
    InitializeAudioCapture();
}

void AMyGameMode::Update(float DeltaTime)
{
    Super::Update(DeltaTime);
}
```

That’s all you need to get a working `AudioCapture` inside a **GameMode** in Unreal Engine 5! 🎧🚀 Feel free to expand it—add UI controls, save recordings, integrate with the audio subsystem, etc. Happy coding!

